/* data/detail-c/mth-12-07.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/mth-12-07.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-07"] = {"mth-12-07-m01":{"idea":"One line splits the plane into two halves, and the inequation picks one half.","steps":["The line ax + by = c cuts the plane into two sides.","Points of one side satisfy ax + by ≤ c, points of the other side do not.","The line itself is included because ≤ allows equality. So the graph is a half plane with its boundary line."],"others":"The line alone would be the graph of the equation, not the inequation. A strip needs two parallel lines. A circle needs a squared equation.","trap":"An inequation gives a region, not a line.","answer":"a half plane, bounded by the line ax + by = c"},"mth-12-07-m02":{"idea":"x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0 keep both coordinates non-negative.","steps":["x ≥ 0 keeps the points on or to the right of the y-axis.","y ≥ 0 keeps the points on or above the x-axis.","Both together give the first quadrant including the two axes."],"others":"The second quadrant has negative x. Below the x-axis has negative y. The whole plane means no restriction at all.","trap":"Non-negativity means the first quadrant.","answer":"the first quadrant, with its boundary axes"},"mth-12-07-m03":{"idea":"The objective function is the quantity you want to make as large or as small as possible.","steps":["It is a linear expression in the variables, like profit Z = 20x + 30y.","You maximise it for profit and minimise it for cost.","The restrictions on the variables are a separate part, called constraints."],"others":"The end of the region is just its boundary. The set of inequations is the constraints. A quadratic function is not allowed in linear programming.","trap":"Objective = what you optimise.","answer":"the linear function which is to be maximised or minimised"},"mth-12-07-m04":{"idea":"Constraints are the limits on resources, written as linear inequations.","steps":["Time, material and money are limited in real problems.","Each limit becomes an inequation like x + 2y ≤ 40.","Together with x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0 they define the feasible region."],"others":"The quantity to optimise is the objective function. Values of Z at vertices are used to compare, not to restrict. Corner points come from the constraints, they are not the constraints.","trap":"Constraints restrict, the objective optimises.","answer":"linear inequations (or equations) which the variables must satisfy"},"mth-12-07-m05":{"idea":"Use the origin as a test point when the line does not pass through it.","steps":["Put x = 0 and y = 0 in the inequation.","If it is true, shade the side with the origin.","If it is false, shade the other side."],"others":"A point on the line always satisfies the equation, so it cannot tell the sides apart. Shading away from the origin is not a rule. Larger x does not decide the side.","trap":"The test point must be off the line.","answer":"test the origin, and shade its side if the inequality holds there"},"mth-12-07-m06":{"idea":"A point is feasible only if it passes every constraint.","steps":["Each constraint gives a shaded half plane.","The common part of all of them, with the first quadrant, is the feasible region.","A point outside even one half plane breaks a constraint."],"others":"Where Z is largest is the optimal point, not the whole region. Corner points are only part of the region. Satisfying at least one constraint is too weak.","trap":"All constraints together, not any one.","answer":"the set of all points that satisfy every constraint together"},"mth-12-07-m07":{"idea":"A linear function on a bounded region has its extremes at the vertices.","steps":["Z is linear, so it changes steadily as you move across the region.","It cannot have a peak or a dip inside, so the extremes are on the edge, at a vertex.","Find Z at every corner point and compare the values."],"others":"The origin is only one of the corners. An interior point is never an extreme for a linear function. The middle of a boundary line is also not special.","trap":"Check every vertex, not only the origin.","answer":"at corner points (vertices) of the feasible region"},"mth-12-07-m08":{"idea":"Test the origin for 2x + 3y ≤ 6.","steps":["At (0, 0): 2(0) + 3(0) = 0.","0 ≤ 6 is true, so the origin satisfies the inequation.","So shade the side of the line that contains the origin."],"others":"The line x + y = 6 is a different line. The line alone is its boundary, not the region. The other side would be wrong because the origin passes.","trap":"True at the origin means shade its side.","answer":"the side that contains the origin"},"mth-12-07-m09":{"idea":"Convex means no dent: a segment between two points stays inside.","steps":["Pick any two points of the set.","Join them by a straight segment.","If the segment is always inside the set, the set is convex."],"others":"Curved boundaries do not decide convexity, since a circle is convex. Containing the origin is unrelated. Having no corner points is not the definition either.","trap":"Think of a star shape, which is not convex.","answer":"the line segment joining any two points of the set lies wholly in the set"},"mth-12-07-m10":{"idea":"A bounded non-empty region has finitely many corners, so Z has a largest and a smallest value there.","steps":["The region is closed and bounded, with a finite number of vertices.","Z has a value at each vertex.","The biggest of those values is the maximum and the smallest is the minimum."],"others":"Having neither contradicts the corner point theorem. Having only a maximum is possible only for an unbounded region. A constant Z happens only for special objective functions.","trap":"Bounded means both exist.","answer":"has both a maximum value and a minimum value"},"mth-12-07-m11":{"idea":"Find where the line meets each axis, then add the origin.","steps":["Put y = 0 in x + y = 4 to get the point (4, 0).","Put x = 0 to get the point (0, 4).","With x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0, the region is a triangle with the origin as the third vertex."],"others":"The point (4, 4) has x + y = 8, which is outside. The set with (4, 4) is not the region. The small triangle with (1, 0) and (0, 1) is for x + y ≤ 1.","trap":"Check each vertex satisfies x + y ≤ 4.","answer":"(0, 0), (4, 0) and (0, 4)"},"mth-12-07-m12":{"idea":"Put each vertex into Z = x + y and compare.","steps":["At (0, 0), Z = 0.","At (4, 0), Z = 4, and at (0, 4), Z = 4.","The largest value is 4."],"others":"Z = 2 is the value at the point (1, 1) or at (2, 0), not the maximum. Z = 8 would need x + y = 8, outside the region. Z = 0 is the minimum.","trap":"Take the largest of all vertex values.","answer":"4"},"mth-12-07-m13":{"idea":"Work out Z = 3x + 2y at the four corners.","steps":["At (0, 0): Z = 0. At (3, 0): Z = 9.","At (3, 1): Z = 9 + 2 = 11. At (0, 4): Z = 8.","The largest is 11."],"others":"8 is the value at only one corner. 12 and 9 do not match any combination of the corners except 9, which is not the largest.","trap":"Do not stop at the first corner.","answer":"11"},"mth-12-07-m14":{"idea":"When two vertices give the same best value, the whole edge between them does too.","steps":["Z = x + y equals 4 at both (4, 0) and (0, 4).","Every point on the line x + y = 4 also has Z = 4.","So the maximum is reached all along that edge."],"others":"At the origin, Z = 0. The point (2, 2) is on the edge, but it is not the only one. The maximum is certainly reached somewhere in the region.","trap":"Equal vertex values mean many optimal points.","answer":"at every point of the segment joining (4, 0) and (0, 4)"},"mth-12-07-m15":{"idea":"Time used cannot exceed time available.","steps":["Product A uses 1 hour per unit, so x units use x hours.","Product B uses 2 hours per unit, so y units use 2y hours.","Total x + 2y can be at most 40, so x + 2y ≤ 40."],"others":"The form with 2x + y swaps the hours of the products. An equation only would force full use of the machine. The form with ≥ would demand at least 40 hours.","trap":"At most means ≤.","answer":"x + 2y ≤ 40"},"mth-12-07-m16":{"idea":"A minimum requirement means the supply must be at least that much.","steps":["x kg of the first food gives x units, y kg of the second gives 2y units.","The total x + 2y must be 8 or more.","So the constraint is x + 2y ≥ 8."],"others":"Setting it to 0 makes no sense. The form with 2x + y mixes up the nutrient amounts. The form with ≤ is for a limit on a resource, not a need.","trap":"At least means ≥.","answer":"x + 2y ≥ 8"},"mth-12-07-m17":{"idea":"A feasible solution only needs to obey the constraints.","steps":["Any point in the feasible region passes every constraint.","It need not give the best value of Z.","The best one is the optimal solution."],"others":"Passing only non-negativity ignores the other constraints. Being on the x-axis does not make a point feasible. The point of maximum Z is the optimal solution, which is a special feasible solution.","trap":"Feasible does not mean best.","answer":"any point that satisfies all the constraints"},"mth-12-07-m18":{"idea":"Optimal means feasible and best.","steps":["Take all feasible solutions.","The one with the greatest Z (for a maximum problem) or the least Z (for a minimum problem) is optimal.","It is found at a corner point."],"others":"A point outside the region cannot be a solution. Any feasible point is only feasible, not necessarily optimal. The origin may not even give the best value.","trap":"Optimal is a feasible point with the best Z.","answer":"a feasible solution that gives the best (largest or smallest) value of the objective function"},"mth-12-07-m19":{"idea":"Test the origin in x + y ≥ 3.","steps":["At (0, 0), x + y = 0.","Is 0 ≥ 3? No, it is false.","So the origin is not in the solution, and the other side is shaded."],"others":"Shading the origin side would need the test to pass. The line alone ignores the half plane. A true statement about 0 is not available here.","trap":"False at the origin means the other side.","answer":"the side that does not contain the origin, since 0 ≥ 3 is false"},"mth-12-07-m20":{"idea":"Sketch the square and see how the slanted line cuts it.","steps":["Without x + y ≤ 6 the region is the square with corner (4, 4).","The line x + y = 6 passes through (4, 2) and (2, 4), cutting off the corner (4, 4).","Corners are (0, 0), (4, 0), (4, 2), (2, 4) and (0, 4), which makes 5."],"others":"3 or 4 corners are too few for this shape. 6 would need an extra cut. The cut replaces one corner by two, so a square's 4 becomes 5.","trap":"One corner removed, two added.","answer":"5"},"mth-12-07-m21":{"idea":"With positive coefficients, Z grows when x or y grows, so the minimum is at a corner.","steps":["Moving away from the origin makes Z larger, because coefficients are positive.","So Z cannot go lower far out, and the smallest value is at a corner.","Corners (3, 0) and (0, 3) give Z = 6 and 9, so the minimum is 6."],"others":"The origin is not in the region. The region does have a minimum because Z has a lower bound. Far points give large values, not the minimum.","trap":"Unbounded does not mean no minimum.","answer":"exists, and it is found at a corner point of the region"},"mth-12-07-m22":{"idea":"Set one variable to zero at a time to get the intercepts.","steps":["Put y = 0: 2x = 10, so x = 5.","Put x = 0: y = 10.","The points are (5, 0) and (0, 10)."],"others":"The point (2, 0) and (0, 1) fail the equation. The pair (10, 0) and (0, 5) swaps the divisions. The pair (5, 0) and (0, 5) uses 5 for both, forgetting the coefficient of y.","trap":"Divide by the coefficient of the variable that stays.","answer":"(5, 0) and (0, 10)"},"mth-12-07-m23":{"idea":"Profit is units times profit per unit, added over products.","steps":["Product A gives ₹20 per unit, so x units give 20x.","Product B gives ₹30 per unit, so y units give 30y.","Total profit Z = 20x + 30y, and a profit is to be maximised."],"others":"50(x + y) uses the same profit for both. The form with 30x + 20y swaps the profits. Minimising a profit makes no sense.","trap":"Profit goes with maximise.","answer":"Z = 20x + 30y, to be maximised"},"mth-12-07-m24":{"idea":"A redundant constraint is one the others already imply.","steps":["Take the other constraints and draw their region.","If this constraint cuts nothing from that region, it adds no restriction.","Removing it leaves the feasible region unchanged."],"others":"Being satisfied only by the origin is a different property. Being an equation is not the definition. Making the region empty would be an infeasible problem.","trap":"Redundant means no effect on the region.","answer":"removing it does not change the feasible region"},"mth-12-07-m25":{"idea":"The sign ≤ includes equality, so the boundary line belongs to the region.","steps":["On the line, ax + by equals c.","c ≤ c is true.","So points on the boundary satisfy the constraint."],"others":"Corner points are found exactly where two boundary lines meet, so boundary points can be corners. The origin is not required. Points on the line do satisfy the constraint.","trap":"Closed inequality includes the boundary.","answer":"satisfies the constraint, as the equality holds"},"mth-12-07-n01":{"idea":"Work out Z at each corner of the region and take the largest.","steps":["Corners are (0, 0), (3, 0), (3, 1) and (0, 4).","Z = 3x + 2y gives 0, 9, 11 and 8.","The maximum is 11, at (3, 1)."],"trap":"Check all four corners.","answer":"11"},"mth-12-07-n02":{"idea":"The region is unbounded, but the minimum is still at a corner.","steps":["Corners are (3, 0) and (0, 3).","Z = 5x + 4y gives 15 at (3, 0) and 12 at (0, 3).","The minimum value is 12."],"trap":"Positive coefficients keep the minimum at a corner.","answer":"12"},"mth-12-07-n03":{"idea":"Find the meeting point of the two lines, then test all corners.","steps":["Solve 2x + y = 10 and x + 2y = 8 to get x = 4, y = 2.","Corners are (0, 0), (5, 0), (4, 2) and (0, 4).","Z = 40x + 50y gives 0, 200, 260 and 200. The maximum is 260."],"trap":"Do not forget the intersection corner.","answer":"260"},"mth-12-07-n04":{"idea":"Minimise the cost at the corners of the unbounded region.","steps":["The lines x + 2y = 8 and 3x + y = 9 meet at (2, 3).","Corners are (8, 0), (2, 3) and (0, 9).","C = 2x + 3y gives 16, 13 and 27. The minimum is 13."],"trap":"The minimum is the smallest corner value.","answer":"13"},"mth-12-07-n05":{"idea":"Count the corners of the square after the slanted cut.","steps":["Without the slanted line, the square has corners up to (4, 4).","The line x + y = 6 cuts off (4, 4) and gives (4, 2) and (2, 4).","Corners are (0, 0), (4, 0), (4, 2), (2, 4) and (0, 4), so there are 5."],"trap":"One corner removed, two added.","answer":"5"},"mth-12-07-n06":{"idea":"Turn the words into constraints, then use corner points.","steps":["Maximise Z = 20x + 30y subject to x + 2y ≤ 40 and 3x + y ≤ 45.","The lines meet at (10, 15). Corners are (0, 0), (15, 0), (10, 15) and (0, 20).","Z = 0, 300, 650 and 600, so the maximum profit is 650."],"trap":"Machine 2 allows at most 15 units of the first product alone.","answer":"650"},"mth-12-07-n07":{"idea":"Compare Z at every vertex.","steps":["Corners are (0, 0), (4, 0), (4, 1) and (0, 5).","Z = 4x + y gives 0, 16, 17 and 5.","The maximum value is 17, at (4, 1)."],"trap":"The heavy coefficient on x pushes the best point to x = 4.","answer":"17"},"mth-12-07-n08":{"idea":"Find the meeting point and test all corners.","steps":["Solve 2x + y = 10 and x + 3y = 15 to get x = 3, y = 4.","Corners are (15, 0), (3, 4) and (0, 10).","Z = 6x + 4y gives 90, 34 and 40. The minimum is 34."],"trap":"The middle corner often gives the minimum.","answer":"34"},"mth-12-07-n09":{"idea":"Count the lattice points row by row.","steps":["For x = 0, y can be 0 to 4, which is 5 points.","For x = 1, 2, 3 and 4, there are 4, 3, 2 and 1 points.","Total = 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 15."],"trap":"Whole-number points only, including those on the line.","answer":"15"}};
